.TH KOPPEL 1 "November 2026" "Koppel" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
koppel \- share USB devices over the network
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B koppel
.RI [ option ...]
.br
.B koppel reset\-password
.br
.B koppel healthcheck
.br
.B koppel diagnostics
.RI [ file ]
.br
.B koppel \-\-help
.br
.B koppel \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B koppel
shares USB devices from a server to clients over USB/IP, carried over TLS, with a web UI.
The package runs it as the
.B koppel
systemd service, which starts at boot.
Open the UI at
.I https://localhost:5240/
and set the password there within ten minutes of the first start.
The page asks for a setup token, which
.B journalctl \-u koppel
shows.
The browser warns about Koppel's own certificate the first time.
.PP
With no command, it runs the service in the foreground, which is what the unit does.
Stop it with Ctrl+C or SIGTERM.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B reset\-password
Clears the UI password and ends every signed-in session, then exits.
Open the UI afterwards and set a new password within the setup window.
Run it as root.
.TP
.B healthcheck
Asks a running Koppel's
.I /healthz
and exits 0 when it answers and 1 when it does not.
It is the Docker image's health check.
.TP
.B diagnostics \fR[\fIfile\fR]
Writes a report for a support request: the version, the operating system, the folders, the config files by name and size,
the plain ones with every value that could be a secret removed, the state of the USB/IP kernel modules and the end of the log.
The encrypted files and the keys are never read.
The report goes to
.I file
or, without one, to standard output.
Run it as root.
.PP
0.1.x's
.B \-\-reset\-password
and
.B \-\-healthcheck
still work, with a deprecation note in the log.
.SH OPTIONS
Every setting is an option and an environment variable, and the option wins:
.B \-\-ui\-port 5250
or
.BR KOPPEL_UI_PORT=5250 .
Under systemd, put the variables in
.I /etc/default/koppel
and restart the service.
The settings include
.BR \-\-ui\-port ,
.BR \-\-network\-port ,
.BR \-\-listen\-address ,
.BR \-\-ui\-allow\-from ,
.BR \-\-config\-dir ,
.BR \-\-keys\-dir
and
.BR \-\-discovery\-enabled .
.B koppel \-\-help
lists them all, and
.B koppel \-\-version
prints the version, the git commit and the build date.
0.1.x's names, such as
.B Ports__Ui
in the environment, still work and are logged as deprecated.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/lib/koppel/config
Settings, devices and the encrypted secret files.
Only root may read it.
.TP
.I /var/lib/koppel/keys
What seals the encrypted files.
Back it up together with the config folder.
.TP
.I /etc/default/koppel
Optional settings for the service, as environment variables.
.TP
.I /etc/koppel/key
Optional.
A key file kept outside
.I /var/lib/koppel
that seals the encrypted files instead of the key in the keys folder.
Uncomment
.B LoadCredential=koppel-key:/etc/koppel/key
in the service unit to use it, or set
.B KOPPEL_KEY_FILE
to another path.
Back it up apart from the config folder.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/koppel.service
The service unit.
.SH LOGS
The service logs to the journal:
.B journalctl \-u koppel
Set
.B RUST_LOG
in
.I /etc/default/koppel
for more, for example
.B RUST_LOG=debug
or
.BR warn,koppel_web=debug .
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success or a clean stop, 1 on an error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR journalctl (1)
.PP
